<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Macedonia (region)</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Macedonia is a historical region of the central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a>, centred on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vardar">Vardar</a> and Aliakmon valleys and the plain around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaloniki">Thessaloniki</a>. It takes its name from the ancient kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-macedon">Macedon</a> and later formed a Roman province, a contested borderland between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a>, and part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> until 1912–1913. It is now divided mainly between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-macedonia">North Macedonia</a> and Bulgaria.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/macedonia-region.jpg" alt="Macedonia (region)" width="320" height="184" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Пакко · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Borders_of_Macedonia_according_authors_(1843-1927).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Society was transformed in the 1920s by the arrival of over a million refugees from Asia Minor and Eastern Thrace, who settled in new suburbs of Athens and Thessaloniki and on land in <b>Macedonia</b> distributed by agrarian reform.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-greece">Kingdom of Greece · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The agrarian reform begun in 1919 abolished the remaining serf-like tenancies in Bosnia and <b>Macedonia</b> and redistributed large estates, but productivity stayed low and rural overpopulation severe; in 1921 about half the adult population was illiterate.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-yugoslavia">Kingdom of Yugoslavia · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Anarchists took part in the Strandzha Commune and the Krusevo Republic of 1903 in <b>Macedonia</b> and in the Mexican Revolution of 1910.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/anarchism">Anarchism · Spread &amp; Variants</a></li><li><blockquote>The Berziti were among the Slavic groups that settled in <b>Macedonia</b> in the 7th century, in the region around Ohrid, Prilep and Bitola.</blockquote><a href="/state/berziti">Berziti</a></li><li><blockquote>During the Marcomannic Wars, in about 170 CE, they broke through the Danube frontier, swept through Moesia, Thrace and <b>Macedonia</b>, and plundered the mysteries sanctuary at Eleusis near Athens.</blockquote><a href="/state/costoboci">Costoboci</a></li><li><blockquote>They raided <b>Macedonia</b> repeatedly and took advantage of the Celtic invasions of 279 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/dardanian-kingdom">Dardanian Kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
